\description{
\code{geom_ppplot()} and \code{geom_qqplot()} create one-sample probability-probability
and quantile-quantile diagnostic layers for comparing a sample to a fully
specified null distribution. Both draw the order-statistic points, an optional
identity line, and a simultaneous 95\% DKW/Massart confidence band by default.
}
\details{
Suppose \eqn{X_1, \ldots, X_n} are compared against a fully specified null
distribution \eqn{F_0}, with quantile function
\eqn{Q_0 = F_0^{\leftarrow}}. Let
\eqn{x_{(1)} \le \cdots \le x_{(n)}} denote the sample order statistics and
let \eqn{p_i} be the plotting positions returned by \code{\link[stats:ppoints]{stats::ppoints()}}, which
by default are \eqn{p_i = (i - 0.5) / n}.

A probability-probability (PP) plot displays
\eqn{F_0(x_{(i)})} against \eqn{p_i}. A quantile-quantile (QQ) plot displays
\eqn{x_{(i)}} against \eqn{Q_0(p_i)}. In both cases, agreement with the null
model is represented by the identity line \eqn{y = x}. Michael (1983)
discusses acceptance regions for PP, QQ, and stabilized probability plots.

The confidence band is based on the probability integral transform: under
\eqn{H_0: F_X = F_0}, the transformed observations
\eqn{U_i = F_0(X_i)} are iid \eqn{\mathrm{Uniform}(0, 1)}. The
Dvoretzky--Kiefer--Wolfowitz/Massart inequality gives, with
\eqn{\alpha = 1 - \mathrm{level}},
\deqn{
  \varepsilon_{n,\alpha} =
  \sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\alpha)}{2n}} .
}
Thus the PP band is drawn on the probability scale as
\deqn{
  \max(0, p - \varepsilon_{n,\alpha})
  \le F_0(x_{(i)}) \le
  \min(1, p + \varepsilon_{n,\alpha}),
}
and the QQ band is obtained by transforming these probability limits back to
the data scale:
\deqn{
  Q_0\{\max(0, p - \varepsilon_{n,\alpha})\}
  \le x_{(i)} \le
  Q_0\{\min(1, p + \varepsilon_{n,\alpha})\}.
}
QQ ribbons are evaluated on a finite probability grid that reaches halfway
from the extreme plotting positions toward 0 and 1, so the ribbon extends
slightly beyond the observed points on the theoretical-quantile axis. In
\code{geom_qqplot()}, the ribbon coordinates are drawn after scale training, so
the default x and y scales are determined by the QQ points rather than by the
ribbon tails.

Points far from the identity line, and especially points outside the band,
are visual evidence against the specified null distribution. The band is
finite-sample and distribution-free for a fully specified \eqn{F_0}; if
parameters are estimated from the same data, the display is best interpreted
as an informal diagnostic. The DKW bands are known to be conservative, so
power can be expected to be low.
}
\section{Computed variables}{

These are calculated by the \code{stat} part of the layer and can be accessed
with \code{\link[ggplot2:aes_eval]{ggplot2::after_stat()}}.
\describe{
\item{\code{after_stat(x)}}{Theoretical probabilities for PP plots or
theoretical quantiles for QQ plots.}
\item{\code{after_stat(y)}}{Observed probabilities for PP plots or observed
sample quantiles for QQ plots.}
\item{\code{after_stat(p)}}{Plotting positions returned by \code{\link[stats:ppoints]{stats::ppoints()}}.}
\item{\code{after_stat(theoretical)}}{Theoretical probabilities or quantiles.}
\item{\code{after_stat(observed)}}{Observed probabilities or sample quantiles.}
\item{\code{after_stat(sample)}}{Sorted sample values.}
\item{\code{after_stat(n)}}{Sample size after removing non-finite values.}
\item{\code{after_stat(ymin)} and \code{after_stat(ymax)}}{Lower and upper confidence
band limits when \code{conf_int = TRUE}.}
}
}

\section{Aesthetics}{

\code{geom_ppplot()} and \code{geom_qqplot()} require the following aesthetic:
\describe{
\item{\code{x}}{Observed sample values.}
}
They also understand point aesthetics such as \code{alpha}, \code{colour}/\code{color},
\code{fill}, \code{group}, \code{shape}, \code{size}, and \code{stroke}; confidence-band aesthetics
such as \code{fill} and \code{alpha}; and identity-line parameters supplied through
\code{line_color}, \code{line_linetype}, and \code{line_linewidth}.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
df <- data.frame(x = rnorm(50))

ggplot(df, aes(x = x)) +
  geom_ppplot(fun = pnorm) +
  coord_equal()

ggplot(df, aes(x = x)) +
  geom_qqplot(fun = qnorm) +
  coord_equal()

# Use fixed black points by setting a fixed colour.
ggplot(df, aes(x = x)) +
  geom_qqplot(fun = qnorm, colour = "black") +
  coord_equal()

# Or add a spectral/rainbow colour scale explicitly.
ggplot(df, aes(x = x)) +
  geom_qqplot(fun = qnorm) +
  scale_colour_gradientn(colors = grDevices::rainbow(10)) +
  coord_equal()


}
